{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792670529,"nonce":"BPL0wS5XB6YjBkFYoiSHLLuYcio=","title":"Explorer rows are not virtualized: opening folders costs about 200 KB of heap per row","message":"Measured with tests/ExplorerMemoryProbe.hx on this project (AOT, vmmap footprint, GC heap after a major collection), three runs each: the workbench with the Explorer closed to its root is 145-150 MiB. Opening all 135 folders (1,672 rows, 10,438 layout nodes) makes it 642-647 MiB, heap 392-398 MB against 47. That is about 210 KB of heap for each row.\n\nWhy: ashui's TreeView loads a folder's items the first time it opens, but then keeps every item mounted, and closing a folder only folds it to no height; its items stay. Nothing is windowed to the viewport. A row is five layout nodes, two Svg elements, a Text, interaction and layout-animation state, and signals.\n\nWhat a piece costs (heap per instance, same probe): Signal 0.13 KB, Computed 0.35 KB, Watch 0.3 KB, so 10,000 of them are 1-3 MB and signals are not the cause. Empty Div 6.3 KB, Svg 8.5 KB, Text about 21 KB (the largest part, likely native text measurement), focusable +4 KB, layout animation +2.5 KB. A bare tree-item of 1,600 plain rows is 195 MB, 122 KB each.\n\nFile type icon documents are cheap (67 documents, 96 KB).\n\nWanted: the Explorer shows only the rows in view, from a flat list of the visible entries, pooled and reused as the code editor's rows are; and a folder that is closed releases its rows. Either a virtual mode in ashui's TreeView, or a RayDE tree over a flat model. The Text per row is the first thing to cut.","files":null}]}